<!-- WHAT DRIVES COST -->
<section class="cx-section cx-spotlight" style="background:var(--dark)">
  <div class="cx-container">
    <div class="r">
      <div class="cx-eyebrow">Cost Drivers</div>
      <h2 class="cx-h2">Five factors that move your<br>renovation cost the most.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="cx-problem-grid stagger" style="margin-top:48px">
      <div class="cx-problem-card r">
        <h3>1. Existing Conditions</h3>
        <p>Second-generation space (previously built out, MEP in place) costs 25–40% less than a raw shell. If you can take a space that was previously an office and renovate it to an office again, you avoid full MEP rough-in and potentially avoid change-of-use permits.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="cx-problem-card r">
        <h3>2. MEP Scope</h3>
        <p>Mechanical, electrical, and plumbing work is the biggest line item on most commercial renovations. Adding plumbing to a floor that does not have it, upgrading an electrical panel, or running new HVAC branches can each add $15–$40 per square foot to the project cost.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="cx-problem-card r">
        <h3>3. Custom Millwork</h3>
        <p>A reception desk with stone countertop and feature lighting adds $15,000–$40,000 to a project. A law firm boardroom with custom built-ins can add $25,000–$60,000. Millwork is the primary lever between mid-range and high-end finishes.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="cx-problem-card r">
        <h3>4. Finish Specification</h3>
        <p>The difference between carpet tile and polished concrete is $8–$18 per square foot for flooring alone. Acoustic tile ceilings vs open-concept painted ceiling vs suspended feature system ranges from $4 to $22 per square foot. These choices compound across a large space.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="cx-problem-card r">
        <h3>5. Permit Complexity</h3>
        <p>A standard tenant improvement permit in Toronto costs $3,000–$8,000 in fees. A change-of-use permit can cost $8,000–$25,000 in fees and add 6–12 weeks to the schedule, which also adds carrying cost. Medical and assembly occupancies face additional fire and life safety review.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="cx-problem-card r">
        <h3>6. Schedule Compression</h3>
        <p>Fast-track delivery with extended hours, weekend shifts, and larger crews costs 10–20% more than a standard schedule. For most clients, the cost of compression is less than the cost of a delayed opening, lost rent on an unusable space, or a missed regulatory deadline.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

