        <h3 style="font-size:1rem;color:var(--pure);margin-bottom:10px">Does the cost per square foot decrease for larger projects?</h3>
        <p style="color:var(--gray2);line-height:1.75;font-size:.9rem">Partially. Overhead and mobilization costs are spread across more area, so large projects (8,000+ sq ft) can see 5–10% efficiency on some line items. However, MEP complexity typically increases with size, so the per-square-foot cost often stays within the same range. The biggest savings come from scope clarity and avoiding change orders, not from project size.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="border-bottom:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.08);padding-bottom:24px">
        <h3 style="font-size:1rem;color:var(--pure);margin-bottom:10px">What is not included in a per-square-foot renovation quote?</h3>
        <p style="color:var(--gray2);line-height:1.75;font-size:.9rem">Standard commercial renovation quotes from VNG include all construction labour and materials, permits, and general contractor management. Excluded are: furniture and workstations, IT and AV equipment, owner-supplied fixtures (ranges, refrigeration, etc.), signage, and security systems (though conduit and rough-in for all of these are included).</p>
      </div>
      <div style="border-bottom:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.08);padding-bottom:24px">
        <h3 style="font-size:1rem;color:var(--pure);margin-bottom:10px">How do GTA renovation costs compare to 2024?</h3>
        <p style="color:var(--gray2);line-height:1.75;font-size:.9rem">Commercial renovation costs in the GTA increased 8–12% between 2024 and 2026, driven primarily by skilled trade labour rates and drywall and steel stud material costs. Office costs were more stable than restaurant and medical, where MEP labour shortages are most acute. The ranges published here reflect current 2026 market conditions.</p>
